{"causes":["Written by the Christian prophet John in exile on the island of Patmos around 90-110 CE.","Influenced by the historical and cultural context of the Roman Empire and the Jewish diaspora.","Shaped by the theological debates and disputes of the early Christian Church."],"effects":["Influenced the development of Christian theology, particularly in the areas of eschatology and the nature of God.","Informed the rise of Millenarianism and the apocalyptic imagination in Western culture.","Continues to be relevant in modern times, with ongoing debates about its interpretation and meaning."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"new-testament","sourceName":"New Testament","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"book-of-revelation","targetName":"Book of Revelation","context":"The Book of Revelation is the last book of the New Testament canon."},{"sourceSlug":"christianity","sourceName":"Christianity","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-revelation","targetName":"Book of Revelation","context":"The Book of Revelation has shaped the development of Christian theology and its understanding of the end times."},{"sourceSlug":"roman-empire","sourceName":"Roman Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-revelation","targetName":"Book of Revelation","context":"The Book of Revelation was written during the reign of the Roman Empire and reflects the cultural and historical context of that time."},{"sourceSlug":"early-christian-church","sourceName":"Early Christian Church","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-revelation","targetName":"Book of Revelation","context":"The theological debates and disputes of the early Christian Church influenced the writing and interpretation of the Book of Revelation."},{"sourceSlug":"millenarianism","sourceName":"Millenarianism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-revelation","targetName":"Book of Revelation","context":"The Book of Revelation has been seen as a key text in the development of Millenarianism and the apocalyptic imagination in Western culture."},{"sourceSlug":"art-and-literature","sourceName":"Art and Literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-revelation","targetName":"Book of Revelation","context":"The Book of Revelation has inspired countless works of art, from illuminated manuscripts to modern films and literature."},{"sourceSlug":"christian-theology","sourceName":"Christian Theology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-revelation","targetName":"Book of Revelation","context":"The Book of Revelation has shaped the development of Christian theology, particularly in the areas of eschatology and the nature of God."},{"sourceSlug":"jesus-christ","sourceName":"Jesus Christ","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-revelation","targetName":"Book of Revelation","context":"The Book of Revelation is a prophetic text that contains visions and messages from Jesus Christ to the seven churches of Asia."},{"sourceSlug":"asia","sourceName":"Asia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"book-of-revelation","targetName":"Book of Revelation","context":"The Book of Revelation was written during the reign of the Roman Empire and reflects the cultural and historical context of Asia during that time."}],"places":[{"name":"Patmos","role":"place of writing"},{"name":"Asia","role":"cultural and historical context"},{"name":"Rome","role":"influence"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Book of Revelation","type":"Reference article","slug":"book-of-revelation"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q42040","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Revelation"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Book of Revelation left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 140 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Book of Revelation (Greek: Ἀποκάλυψις, Apokalypsis, 'Apocalypse' or 'Unveiling') is the final book of the Christian New Testament — an apocalyptic prophetic text attributed to 'John of Patmos' (traditionally identified with the apostle John, though modern scholarship distinguishes several possible authors) and composed c. 95 CE during the reign of the Roman Emperor Domitian. Written in a highly symbolic and visionary register drawing on Hebrew prophetic literature (particularly Ezekiel, Daniel, and Zechariah), it addresses seven churches of Asia Minor, describes a series of heavenly visions of cosmic war between good and evil, the defeat of Rome (figured as 'Babylon, the great whore'), the Last Judgment, and the establishment of a New Jerusalem — the eternal kingdom of God on earth. The most contested and variously interpreted text in the New Testament, Revelation has exercised an influence on Christian theology, art, literature, and political imagination that is disproportionate to its late and disputed canonical status.

Revelation's key images and narrative sequences — the Four Horsemen of the Apocalypse (War, Famine, Death, Conquest), the Beast of 666, the Whore of Babylon, the Millennium (1,000-year reign of Christ), Armageddon, the New Jerusalem — have become so deeply embedded in Western culture that they shape imagination far beyond explicitly religious contexts. The text was used by early Christians as a coded resistance document against Roman imperial persecution; by medieval millenarian movements (the Cathars, the Joachimites, the Taborites) as prophecy of imminent divine revolution; by the Reformation's radical wing as a critique of papal Rome; by Puritan colonists in America who saw themselves as the New Jerusalem; and by 20th–21st century evangelical Protestantism (particularly the Left Behind series and dispensationalist theology) as a literal prophecy of the End Times.

Revelation's political theology — its depiction of earthly empire as demonic, its promise of divine vindication for the persecuted, its vision of history moving toward a cosmic resolution — has made it both a resource for revolutionary resistance (liberation theology, abolitionism) and, in its more literal interpretations, a potential driver of apocalyptic politics.","subjects":["New Testament","Christian Theology","Classical Era","Apocalyptic Literature","Eschatology","Early Christianity","Roman Empire","World Literature"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Book of Revelation has had a profound impact on the development of Christian theology and its understanding of the end times, and continues to be relevant in modern times.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
